The ASAN service centers
have completely changed

00:14:11.433 --> 00:14:14.937
the idea of service delivery
by bringing together

00:14:14.937 --> 00:14:19.441
different vendors and as
many as 300 plus services

00:14:19.441 --> 00:14:20.943
at the one place.

00:14:20.943 --> 00:14:24.238
It has radically changed
how government is delivering

00:14:24.238 --> 00:14:27.074
services at a service center level.

00:14:27.074 --> 00:14:29.702
- Traditionally ways
are not suitable anymore

00:14:29.702 --> 00:14:33.622
to provide our services and
to get satisfaction level.

00:14:33.622 --> 00:14:37.585
But also we know that it's
not only about technologies.

00:14:37.585 --> 00:14:40.588
It's also about organizational structures.

00:14:40.588 --> 00:14:44.216
It's about human resources
and we are trying

00:14:44.216 --> 00:14:47.970
to build suitable environment
in order to provide

00:14:47.970 --> 00:14:51.557
our services effectively and
efficiently to our citizens.

00:14:58.314 --> 00:15:00.190
- Where the government has
placed an incredible emphasis

00:15:00.190 --> 00:15:04.570
on this, is the recognition
that everyone is different.

00:15:04.570 --> 00:15:08.949
And a tremendous experience
is often not something

00:15:08.949 --> 00:15:11.785
that is entirely frictionless,
like booking a train ticket,

00:15:11.785 --> 00:15:14.705
it's not that difficult to
say what does the station

00:15:14.705 --> 00:15:17.041
you need to go to, what time,
where are you leaving from

00:15:17.041 --> 00:15:18.709
and how many passengers are there.

00:15:18.709 --> 00:15:20.669
That's a very automated process that

00:15:20.669 --> 00:15:23.380
has almost no variations.

00:15:23.380 --> 00:15:25.966
But when you're trying to change your name

00:15:25.966 --> 00:15:28.135
and actually the options that you're going

00:15:28.135 --> 00:15:31.513
for a not available or when
you're trying to consume

00:15:31.513 --> 00:15:34.850
healthcare services and
you have two children

00:15:34.850 --> 00:15:38.062
who are naturally born, one is adopted.

00:15:38.062 --> 00:15:41.774
These are the types of unique
situations that actually,

00:15:41.774 --> 00:15:45.194
when organizations are able
to deal with that effectively

00:15:45.194 --> 00:15:47.863
that's what makes people
feel really good about it.

00:15:47.863 --> 00:15:50.324
Because it makes it feel
like they actually understand

00:15:50.324 --> 00:15:52.785
that there are very
personalized requirements

00:15:52.785 --> 00:15:54.620
that citizens have.

00:15:54.620 --> 00:15:58.707
- If you sit at home, how is
your feeling about the services

00:15:58.707 --> 00:16:00.209
you get from the government?

00:16:00.209 --> 00:16:03.087
Are you worried about
missing out or forgetting

00:16:03.087 --> 00:16:06.048
about paying your bills,
your utilities bills,

00:16:06.048 --> 00:16:08.384
or are you comfortable
because you know that it will

00:16:08.384 --> 00:16:12.763
automatically be processed
through your digital assistant,

00:16:12.763 --> 00:16:15.724
that kind of level, and
that kind of experience

00:16:15.724 --> 00:16:19.186
is what we're seeking, where
a customer does not need

00:16:19.186 --> 00:16:21.355
to worry everything runs in the background

00:16:21.355 --> 00:16:22.940
on their behalf.

00:16:22.940 --> 00:16:24.942
- Consuming government
services anywhere in the world

00:16:24.942 --> 00:16:28.237
is normally met with the
thought of bureaucracy

00:16:28.237 --> 00:16:29.780
and lots of paperwork.

00:16:29.780 --> 00:16:31.865
I think what's different here
is that there has been an

00:16:33.867 --> 00:16:35.035
incredible focus on
making sure that the pain

00:16:35.035 --> 00:16:37.204
and the friction that normally is involved

00:16:37.204 --> 00:16:40.708
with the yearly renewal of
services has been taken away.

00:16:40.708 --> 00:16:44.962
Now whether that is having your
car renewed which is almost

00:16:44.962 --> 00:16:47.506
entirely a digital transactional,

00:16:48.424 --> 00:16:50.718
whether that is year national ID

00:16:50.718 --> 00:16:52.928
or whether that's just
paying for your parking

00:16:52.928 --> 00:16:54.346
through your mobile phone.

00:16:54.346 --> 00:16:56.181
Everything is interlinked.

00:16:56.181 --> 00:17:00.686
And what's very neat is
there is a single portal app

00:17:00.686 --> 00:17:03.313
where you can then go on
and whether it's hospitals

00:17:03.313 --> 00:17:06.900
or funeral parlors or
government service centers,

00:17:06.900 --> 00:17:09.445
all of that information is
not housed in one place.

00:17:20.914 --> 00:17:23.834
- I survey that three
quarters of CEOs think

00:17:23.834 --> 00:17:26.003
that customer experience is very important

00:17:26.003 --> 00:17:27.588
to their business.

00:17:27.588 --> 00:17:29.715
But there's no such survey in government,

00:17:29.715 --> 00:17:32.468
I don't know how many
government leaders think

00:17:32.468 --> 00:17:34.261
the customer experience is top priority,

00:17:34.261 --> 00:17:36.764
but I'll tell you there are 10
million government employees

00:17:36.764 --> 00:17:38.682
and it's not top priority for everyone.

00:17:41.060 --> 00:17:42.895
- So let's say you have to go to a place,

00:17:42.895 --> 00:17:44.813
you have to go to a physical place.

00:17:44.813 --> 00:17:47.483
What's the experience
like when you get there?

00:17:47.483 --> 00:17:49.026
Is it crowded and chaotic?

00:17:49.026 --> 00:17:50.069
Do you know what to do?

00:17:50.069 --> 00:17:52.154
Do you get good customer service?

00:17:52.154 --> 00:17:54.364
Do you talk to people
that are well-informed

00:17:54.364 --> 00:17:57.451
and if they have difficulty
themselves getting you

00:17:57.451 --> 00:17:59.578
the information, do they have
access to information tools

00:17:59.578 --> 00:18:01.747
to help you as a community member.

00:18:01.747 --> 00:18:04.541
I think I see governments
now beginning to think about

00:18:04.541 --> 00:18:07.920
the entire life cycle of a process.

00:18:08.796 --> 00:18:11.340
For example, getting a copy
of your birth certificate.

00:18:11.340 --> 00:18:15.761
When you want to submit
a request for a permit

00:18:15.761 --> 00:18:19.139
which just such a common
city or government activity,

00:18:19.139 --> 00:18:21.225
it shouldn't be as hard as it is today.

00:18:21.225 --> 00:18:23.393
People don't look forward to
that and the experience should

00:18:23.393 --> 00:18:24.228
be better.

00:18:24.228 --> 00:18:26.772
You should be able to
and increasingly request

00:18:26.772 --> 00:18:30.526
a permit online without
having to go to a place

00:18:30.526 --> 00:18:34.655
and take a number and
wait spend half your day

00:18:34.655 --> 00:18:36.323
in the government office.

00:18:36.323 --> 00:18:39.201
- In the United Arab
Emirates we focus on customer

00:18:39.201 --> 00:18:41.829
experience from their life instances.

00:18:41.829 --> 00:18:45.707
So for example, if the
customer has a newborn baby

00:18:45.707 --> 00:18:50.003
we look at that as an
a point of life where

00:18:50.879 --> 00:18:54.800
they need government services
about having a new baby.

00:18:54.800 --> 00:18:56.093
Therefore, we started looking

00:18:56.093 --> 00:18:58.720
at it from a bundling perspective.

00:18:58.720 --> 00:19:02.015
So instead of a customer
looking at which government

00:19:02.015 --> 00:19:06.103
entities or government service
centers they should visit,

00:19:06.103 --> 00:19:07.980
they just know what bundle they want.

00:19:07.980 --> 00:19:10.023
I'll give you an example
of one of the services

00:19:10.023 --> 00:19:12.693
that we have, it's called Mabrouk Ma Yak,

00:19:12.693 --> 00:19:16.113
which means congratulations
on your newborn.

00:19:16.113 --> 00:19:20.242
And it's bundled more than
seven services into one step

00:19:20.242 --> 00:19:24.371
and more than five
payments into one payment.

00:19:24.371 --> 00:19:27.416
And to get everything
related to this newborn

00:19:27.416 --> 00:19:29.459
within three hours now using this bundle.

00:19:32.796 --> 00:19:35.757
- We are now moving from
a milestone shopping model

00:19:35.757 --> 00:19:36.842
to non-stop shop model.

00:19:36.842 --> 00:19:39.970
In our service centers, we are
providing reactive services

00:19:39.970 --> 00:19:43.599
to our citizens, but now we
started launched new project

00:19:43.599 --> 00:19:47.019
which is called my growth,
my government project.

00:19:47.019 --> 00:19:51.023
And it's about providing proactive
services to our citizens.

00:19:51.023 --> 00:19:55.777
For that we are ready widely
using beta in order to predict

00:19:55.777 --> 00:19:57.654
expectations of our citizens.

00:19:57.654 --> 00:20:02.075
And in-house by the government,
we are trying to involve

00:20:02.075 --> 00:20:04.453
the companies to this process as well.

00:20:15.881 --> 00:20:19.176
- As the result of really
rapidly changing world,

00:20:19.176 --> 00:20:22.971
what is changing is also
citizen expectations.

00:20:22.971 --> 00:20:26.516
Citizens today expect a
different level of service

00:20:26.516 --> 00:20:28.227
at a government level.

00:20:28.227 --> 00:20:31.605
The expectations are a
combination of what they're seeing

00:20:31.605 --> 00:20:34.358
in the private sector,
in the retail sector

00:20:34.358 --> 00:20:38.487
in the healthcare sector in a
combination of this is leading

00:20:38.487 --> 00:20:41.865
to the overall citizen need if you will,

00:20:41.865 --> 00:20:43.700
for service excellence.

00:20:43.700 --> 00:20:47.079
Today's citizens, you and
I, we all use smartphones

00:20:47.079 --> 00:20:50.540
and technology to make
decisions, to access services.

00:20:50.540 --> 00:20:54.544
And it's very common just
to expect that every service

00:20:54.544 --> 00:20:57.839
should be available on a cell
phone, it should be available

00:20:57.839 --> 00:21:01.593
on a mobile device, but not
all governments have been able

00:21:01.593 --> 00:21:05.555
to translate services from
a service center to online.

00:21:05.555 --> 00:21:10.269
- Most, so-called governance
agendas are actually about

00:21:10.269 --> 00:21:15.274
governments providing services
to people through technology.

00:21:15.315 --> 00:21:19.152
So we can see government
websites where you can access,

00:21:19.152 --> 00:21:22.823
forms to fill in passports
or licenses or pay tax.

00:21:22.823 --> 00:21:27.035
That is a really interesting
change of management structure.

00:21:27.035 --> 00:21:30.330
In the old days, governments
had lots of civil servants,

00:21:30.330 --> 00:21:33.583
lots of people working for
them to do the paperwork.

00:21:33.583 --> 00:21:37.754
Now the effort and load has
been transferred back down

00:21:37.754 --> 00:21:38.714
to the citizens.

00:21:38.714 --> 00:21:41.383
It's like in the old days, when
you might attend a meeting,

00:21:41.383 --> 00:21:43.969
somebody would send you all
the paperwork in advance.

00:21:43.969 --> 00:21:46.638
You didn't have to do anything,
you just read the paperwork

00:21:46.638 --> 00:21:49.308
and took it to the meeting.

00:21:49.308 --> 00:21:53.603
Now your sent the files digitally by email

00:21:53.603 --> 00:21:55.397
or another online system,

00:21:55.397 --> 00:21:57.774
or even you're expected to go online

00:21:57.774 --> 00:22:00.902
to download those forms
yourself, then read them,

00:22:00.902 --> 00:22:03.280
then perhaps print them
off because you might need

00:22:03.280 --> 00:22:05.490
to take them to the meeting.

00:22:05.490 --> 00:22:08.535
So that is a shift of labor
from governments to citizens

00:22:08.535 --> 00:22:11.872
or from organizations to employees.

00:22:11.872 --> 00:22:14.875
I hate, having to log on to
three or four different sites

00:22:14.875 --> 00:22:17.669
every day to get information,
whereas it could easily

00:22:17.669 --> 00:22:19.129
just be sent to me in another form.

00:22:19.129 --> 00:22:22.674
The standard rhetoric is of
course, that it makes it much

00:22:23.508 --> 00:22:26.011
easier for governments
and citizens to interact

00:22:26.011 --> 00:22:27.804
on key things that they need to do.

00:22:27.804 --> 00:22:32.184
And that undoubtedly have its
advantages, it's very easy now

00:22:32.184 --> 00:22:33.685
to submit a passport application online

00:22:33.685 --> 00:22:35.187
or your tax forms online.

00:22:35.187 --> 00:22:38.023
Second point though, is
and I'll use the example

00:22:38.023 --> 00:22:40.025
of our own government digital service.

00:22:40.025 --> 00:22:42.652
There are many great things about gov.uk.

00:22:42.652 --> 00:22:44.738
However, if you want to find out anything

00:22:44.738 --> 00:22:48.283
from the government website
about a government department

00:22:48.283 --> 00:22:49.701
or what it does, it's almost impossible.

00:22:49.701 --> 00:22:53.580
And I think that is an
indication there's difference

00:22:53.580 --> 00:22:58.043
between government service
provision and the relationship

00:22:58.043 --> 00:23:00.921
between governments and
citizens and the openness

00:23:00.921 --> 00:23:02.631
and the transparency around that.

00:23:11.139 --> 00:23:14.559
- Today I think citizens are co-creators

00:23:14.559 --> 00:23:16.144
in a government experience.

00:23:16.144 --> 00:23:19.106
There's an opportunity for
each of us to be partners

00:23:19.106 --> 00:23:22.609
and help get stuff done,
a greater openness in,

00:23:24.277 --> 00:23:26.655
more progressive cities and
more tools for collaboration

00:23:26.655 --> 00:23:30.075
are allowing community members
in particular to participate

00:23:30.075 --> 00:23:34.204
in everything from
decision-making to design sessions

00:23:34.204 --> 00:23:36.289
even development of solutions.

00:23:37.207 --> 00:23:38.458
There's some very interesting
experiments been done

00:23:38.458 --> 00:23:42.295
with community, co-funding,
identifying funds

00:23:42.295 --> 00:23:45.257
from public sector and private
sector to get projects done

00:23:45.257 --> 00:23:46.633
that has been difficult to get moving

00:23:46.633 --> 00:23:49.177
because of the reliance
purely on public funds.

00:23:49.177 --> 00:23:51.179
If a government makes available data

00:23:51.179 --> 00:23:53.306
and the community member says,
hey I could build a solution

00:23:53.306 --> 00:23:54.683
using that data.

00:23:54.683 --> 00:23:56.685
Wow, that's a great relationship,

00:23:56.685 --> 00:23:57.894
that's a great partnership.

00:23:57.894 --> 00:24:00.397
We have software engineers
now who can step up

00:24:00.397 --> 00:24:03.275
create interesting solutions
or try to make sense of data

00:24:03.275 --> 00:24:05.485
and serve up the analytics
that helps decision

00:24:05.485 --> 00:24:07.028
makers make better decisions.

00:24:07.028 --> 00:24:09.531
That's a relationship
we've never had the tools

00:24:09.531 --> 00:24:11.241
to be able to do before.

00:24:11.241 --> 00:24:14.828
A good democratic government experience

00:24:14.828 --> 00:24:19.166
is not a spectator sport, it's
something you participate in.

00:24:25.922 --> 00:24:28.425
- State of Washington is one
of my favorite performance

00:24:28.425 --> 00:24:30.510
driven organizations.

00:24:30.510 --> 00:24:32.471
There's something called
results Washington.

00:24:32.471 --> 00:24:35.223
Every single metric about state government

00:24:35.223 --> 00:24:38.935
is available online, drill
downs to get greater detail.

00:24:38.935 --> 00:24:41.813
And when the governor
hosts a results meeting,

00:24:41.813 --> 00:24:42.898
the public is invited.

00:24:42.898 --> 00:24:47.611
It's streamed live online, but
they also include a customer

00:24:47.611 --> 00:24:50.030
or two or three in every single meeting.

00:24:50.030 --> 00:24:52.824
So if it's about youth
development, there are actually

00:24:52.824 --> 00:24:55.869
youth they're giving
feedback to the government

00:24:55.869 --> 00:24:59.581
about how the services
are improving, where they

00:24:59.581 --> 00:25:00.749
need more improvement.

00:25:01.583 --> 00:25:02.626
So that's a great example
at the state level.

00:25:02.626 --> 00:25:06.338
And right now, 90% of our
federal government websites

00:25:06.338 --> 00:25:08.131
are collecting customer feedback

00:25:08.131 --> 00:25:10.425
and trying to iteratively improve.

00:25:10.425 --> 00:25:13.595
- My takeaway is that,
it's really interesting

00:25:13.595 --> 00:25:14.387
what's happening in the world today.

00:25:14.387 --> 00:25:17.432
The amount of data that is
generated on a daily basis

00:25:17.432 --> 00:25:19.768
is just mind-boggling.

00:25:19.768 --> 00:25:22.145
Yet you talk to a lot of the enterprises,

00:25:22.145 --> 00:25:24.773
you talk to government, you
talk to a private sector,

00:25:24.773 --> 00:25:27.692
you talk to large corporates
and they will tell you

00:25:27.692 --> 00:25:28.985
that they have poor data,

00:25:28.985 --> 00:25:31.738
issues with quality, there
are gaps in the data.

00:25:31.738 --> 00:25:33.073
There's a problem.

00:25:33.073 --> 00:25:35.784
- There's a lot of legacy
systems and data that's out there

00:25:35.784 --> 00:25:38.828
that's quite difficult to
mine and bring to the surface.

00:25:38.828 --> 00:25:41.581
When you're building something greenfield

00:25:41.581 --> 00:25:44.793
is much more simple to
do that from A to Z.

00:25:44.793 --> 00:25:47.170
But the truth is a lot
of government departments

00:25:47.170 --> 00:25:51.841
have huge legacy of information
systems that they're using

00:25:51.841 --> 00:25:53.635
untraditional processes.

00:25:53.635 --> 00:25:56.263
So the difficulty is trying
to stitch those together

00:25:56.263 --> 00:25:59.140
in a format that the customer
doesn't feel any bumps.

00:25:59.140 --> 00:26:02.519
It's like having a shopping
trolley being pushed across

00:26:02.519 --> 00:26:05.814
a railway track, very bumpy experience.

00:26:05.814 --> 00:26:07.691
And what you're trying to do is take all

00:26:07.691 --> 00:26:09.526
of those knots out of the experience.

00:26:27.127 --> 00:26:30.338
- In all sources you
need a form of identity,

00:26:30.338 --> 00:26:33.341
but it's identity it sounds
like there's only one identity.

00:26:33.341 --> 00:26:36.595
And I expect you have several
identities which you can use.

00:26:36.595 --> 00:26:39.264
So I will use another identity to log in,

00:26:39.264 --> 00:26:41.641
in my for example, my Google accounts

00:26:41.641 --> 00:26:45.562
then I will use for governmental
services for examples.

00:26:45.562 --> 00:26:50.317
I hope these digital
identities, several identities

00:26:50.317 --> 00:26:52.152
are owned by me and are controlled by me.

00:26:52.152 --> 00:26:55.655
- The state of Indiana recently
created a single sign on,

00:26:55.655 --> 00:26:59.075
so that I can get my park
permit and my driver's license,

00:26:59.075 --> 00:27:02.120
certifications, any government
activity that I'm doing

00:27:02.120 --> 00:27:04.289
with the state is all under one ID.

00:27:04.289 --> 00:27:07.959
So that I don't have to
continually reenter my information

00:27:07.959 --> 00:27:10.253
so that's wonderful sort of one-stop shop.

00:27:10.253 --> 00:27:13.548
But, we're still a long way
from being able to integrate

00:27:13.548 --> 00:27:16.176
from the federal to
the state, to the local

00:27:16.176 --> 00:27:17.552
with a single identity.

00:27:17.552 --> 00:27:21.473
And I think it's probably
worth being careful about this,

00:27:21.473 --> 00:27:26.436
because with a single identity
in government comes risk,

00:27:27.812 --> 00:27:31.107
could it be hacked, when it's
done by the private sector

00:27:31.107 --> 00:27:33.443
government doesn't have the control,

00:27:33.443 --> 00:27:37.989
and can't police it in the
way that might be necessary.

00:27:37.989 --> 00:27:42.285
So for example in the us, we
have for the ability to travel,

00:27:42.285 --> 00:27:46.331
the Transportation Security
Administration is responsible

00:27:46.331 --> 00:27:49.417
for issuing TSA pre-check,
so that they vet me

00:27:49.417 --> 00:27:51.294
and then I can go in an express lane.

00:27:51.294 --> 00:27:52.796
Well, there's also a private sector entity

00:27:52.796 --> 00:27:53.797
that's doing that.

00:27:53.797 --> 00:27:56.007
What is the fundamental
role of government?

00:27:56.007 --> 00:27:58.176
Should it be the government
that says who can get

00:27:58.176 --> 00:28:02.055
on a plane and is safe
to travel and who isn't.

00:28:02.055 --> 00:28:05.684
- If you let the government
have all of your identity,

00:28:05.684 --> 00:28:07.602
what might they do with it?

00:28:07.602 --> 00:28:12.357
I often liken this to what
happened in Rwanda, where the use

00:28:12.357 --> 00:28:15.443
of older digital technologies,
radio for example,

00:28:15.443 --> 00:28:18.238
enabled many parts of the genocide there

00:28:18.238 --> 00:28:19.864
to happen more efficiently.

00:28:19.864 --> 00:28:22.826
So if a government knows
everybody's identity

00:28:22.826 --> 00:28:24.869
and where they live and
everything about them

00:28:24.869 --> 00:28:26.413
what can they do with that information.

00:28:26.413 --> 00:28:28.707
For migrants, it's very often issues

00:28:28.707 --> 00:28:31.710
around weather qualifications for example,

00:28:31.710 --> 00:28:33.962
gained in one country can be compatible

00:28:33.962 --> 00:28:35.880
with those games in another country.

00:28:35.880 --> 00:28:38.758
Imagine if everybody
had a digital identity,

00:28:38.758 --> 00:28:42.846
that they could take with
them, so that if you are moving

00:28:42.846 --> 00:28:46.766
from Kenya to South Africa
or from Haiti to Brazil,

00:28:46.766 --> 00:28:49.227
actually you can take
your identity with you.

00:28:49.227 --> 00:28:52.814
I think that raises really
exciting possibilities

00:28:52.814 --> 00:28:57.152
not only for migrants,
but also for governments.

00:28:57.152 --> 00:29:00.238
If we could get global
agreements around what their

00:29:00.238 --> 00:29:02.282
parameters and what the credentials

00:29:02.282 --> 00:29:05.493
in digital identity meant, that
could be really beneficial.

00:29:08.705 --> 00:29:11.458
- So it won't be one
technology which will solve

00:29:11.458 --> 00:29:14.127
the identity topic.

00:29:14.127 --> 00:29:16.796
It will be a combination,
and this moment we really try

00:29:16.796 --> 00:29:20.425
to figure out how can we
combine a digital identity

00:29:20.425 --> 00:29:23.178
with privacy preserving technologies

00:29:23.178 --> 00:29:26.806
like zero knowledge proofs
or multi-party computation.

00:29:26.806 --> 00:29:29.976
I think this is a really
interesting area to explore

00:29:29.976 --> 00:29:31.311
at an international level.

00:29:43.782 --> 00:29:46.367
- We believe in Estonia
that digital identity

00:29:46.367 --> 00:29:48.328
is the backbone of everything.

00:29:48.328 --> 00:29:52.040
Every person should have
an identity match to them

00:29:52.040 --> 00:29:54.334
physically and digitally.

00:29:54.334 --> 00:29:58.004
So, every Estonian communicates
with the government

00:29:58.004 --> 00:30:01.299
using their digital identity
meaning they do everything

00:30:01.299 --> 00:30:05.261
from bank statements, taxes
to registering the childbirth,

00:30:05.261 --> 00:30:07.222
using the digital identity.

00:30:07.222 --> 00:30:10.850
And we really can't imagine
our life any other way.

00:30:10.850 --> 00:30:13.853
- It's been nice actually for
us as Estonian government,

00:30:13.853 --> 00:30:17.315
the learning journey to get
to have experience in focus.

00:30:17.315 --> 00:30:19.734
I'll be very honest here, I
think we've been very techie

00:30:19.734 --> 00:30:22.445
and engineer led throughout
our sort of beginning years

00:30:22.445 --> 00:30:26.741
especially, in a sense that
seeing okay, how much better

00:30:26.741 --> 00:30:29.244
how much more efficient we
can be if we functionally

00:30:29.244 --> 00:30:31.955
build things up and for
example put services online,

00:30:31.955 --> 00:30:33.414
whatever the experience.

00:30:33.414 --> 00:30:35.124
Now we really come to
the point that took it

00:30:35.124 --> 00:30:37.669
to the next level, obviously
experience has to be

00:30:37.669 --> 00:30:39.462
at the forefront, because
otherwise we don't have people

00:30:39.462 --> 00:30:40.713
as using it.

00:30:40.713 --> 00:30:45.510
And, so it goes off course way
beyond just issues of nice,

00:30:45.510 --> 00:30:47.637
neat design of an online environment.

00:30:47.637 --> 00:30:51.975
Our core focuses is how do we
make the process, the working

00:30:51.975 --> 00:30:54.936
of government into the best experience.

00:30:54.936 --> 00:30:57.647
And in often case for
us just to look forward,

00:30:57.647 --> 00:31:01.484
I mean, it's about how
do we simplify radically

00:31:01.484 --> 00:31:02.986
because the best
experience, at least as far

00:31:02.986 --> 00:31:04.612
as the student is
concerned, if there's none,

00:31:04.612 --> 00:31:06.447
if that happens for me.

00:31:06.447 --> 00:31:09.200
As an Estonian, it's
very hard to give advice

00:31:09.200 --> 00:31:10.243
and physical service centers,

00:31:10.243 --> 00:31:13.830
because for us digital
journey has meant getting

00:31:13.830 --> 00:31:16.374
rid of them largely.

00:31:16.374 --> 00:31:18.960
- There is the tax board
that has a physical standard

00:31:18.960 --> 00:31:20.753
and perhaps the post.

00:31:20.753 --> 00:31:23.715
But otherwise, if we talk
about governmental services

00:31:23.715 --> 00:31:25.675
they are 99% online.

00:31:25.675 --> 00:31:29.262
- If you can't get access
to digital in whatever means

00:31:29.262 --> 00:31:30.972
because you don't have connectivity,

00:31:30.972 --> 00:31:32.557
you don't have electricity.

00:31:32.557 --> 00:31:35.226
You're gonna be further
marginalized by the delivery

00:31:35.226 --> 00:31:37.228
of digital services.

00:31:37.228 --> 00:31:39.898
The classic example is around
the notion of smart cities.

00:31:39.898 --> 00:31:42.358
I often say just by saying
the word smart cities,

00:31:42.358 --> 00:31:44.736
actually implies villages are fairly dumb.

00:31:44.736 --> 00:31:47.572
So one of the main challenges is very much

00:31:47.572 --> 00:31:50.116
that you can't deliver say
what you can in a small

00:31:50.116 --> 00:31:53.077
densely populated area
that has full connectivity.

00:31:53.077 --> 00:31:56.080
That's why it's so
important to have access

00:31:56.080 --> 00:31:57.665
in the first instance.

00:31:57.665 --> 00:32:00.251
- And not all our citizens
are technologically literate.

00:32:00.251 --> 00:32:02.712
That's why we need us on service centers

00:32:02.712 --> 00:32:05.757
in all parts of our country.

00:32:05.757 --> 00:32:07.508
Also, we have mobile buses.

00:32:07.508 --> 00:32:10.511
This buses are specially equipped

00:32:10.511 --> 00:32:12.180
with different technologies.

00:32:12.180 --> 00:32:15.433
We sent that buses to the remote areas

00:32:15.433 --> 00:32:17.393
where we don't have ASAS
service centers in order

00:32:17.393 --> 00:32:19.395
to serve our citizens.

00:32:19.395 --> 00:32:23.650
And also we have volunteers.

00:32:23.650 --> 00:32:27.320
- The human factor within
the government experience

00:32:27.320 --> 00:32:28.488
is a primary factor.

00:32:28.488 --> 00:32:30.615
From all perspectives, I would say.

00:32:30.615 --> 00:32:32.867
So if it is a, from a customer perspective

00:32:32.867 --> 00:32:35.286
or from a government employee perspective,

00:32:35.286 --> 00:32:36.454
even the employees who work

00:32:36.454 --> 00:32:39.958
on delivering government services,

00:32:39.958 --> 00:32:41.709
we looked at their happiness level.

00:32:42.752 --> 00:32:46.255
We looked at how they
understand what customers want.

00:32:46.255 --> 00:32:48.800
Therefore, we have conducted
a series of trainings.

00:32:48.800 --> 00:32:51.636
It's a customer happiness
training, where we look

00:32:51.636 --> 00:32:54.973
at the frontline employees
who provide the services.

00:32:54.973 --> 00:32:59.102
We trained them on how to
conduct business in a manner

00:32:59.102 --> 00:33:01.270
that will make the
customer happier and also

00:33:01.270 --> 00:33:03.940
at the same time, we looked
at the supervisors of these

00:33:03.940 --> 00:33:06.401
government service centers which we call

00:33:06.401 --> 00:33:09.237
the United Arab Emirates, the
customer happiness center.

00:33:09.237 --> 00:33:11.906
And we trained them on how to supervise

00:33:11.906 --> 00:33:14.367
a team of customer happiness officers.

00:33:14.367 --> 00:33:17.704
- I'm not in favor of
absolutely 100% abolishing

00:33:17.704 --> 00:33:21.624
every government service
physically, because we see

00:33:21.624 --> 00:33:24.877
that some regions for Estonia,
this is the kind of only

00:33:24.877 --> 00:33:27.088
form of communication
that some of these people

00:33:27.088 --> 00:33:30.299
actually have and they enjoy
these forms of communication.

00:33:30.299 --> 00:33:32.343
They enjoy going to the post office,

00:33:32.343 --> 00:33:36.806
but in terms of city life, in
terms of actually going to the

00:33:36.806 --> 00:33:38.975
tax office and waiting
three hours to fill in

00:33:38.975 --> 00:33:41.811
the stack of paper, that
should be eliminated absolute.

00:33:49.652 --> 00:33:52.780
- The way the shape government
services is really clear.

00:33:52.780 --> 00:33:56.451
We need across the board,
a template of excellence.

00:33:56.451 --> 00:33:59.495
We need something that
can be easily adapted,

00:33:59.495 --> 00:34:00.747
easily deployed.

00:34:00.747 --> 00:34:04.792
For example, if a country
has a model of excellence

00:34:04.792 --> 00:34:08.337
is that model able to be
replicated across different groups,

00:34:08.337 --> 00:34:10.673
across different
departments and ministries.

00:34:10.673 --> 00:34:12.717
That's exactly what is needed.

00:34:12.717 --> 00:34:15.553
The GX program in the United Arab Emirates

00:34:15.553 --> 00:34:17.764
is an excellent example of that.

00:34:17.764 --> 00:34:21.184
Government service
excellence is a top priority

00:34:21.184 --> 00:34:23.519
for the government of the UAE.

00:34:23.519 --> 00:34:26.105
Things such as tweeting
about the rankings of

00:34:26.105 --> 00:34:29.692
government service centers is
not very uncommon in the UAE,

00:34:29.692 --> 00:34:32.904
and this is how leadership
runs the country.

00:34:32.904 --> 00:34:36.365
- GX was created with the
vision and mind to drive

00:34:36.365 --> 00:34:40.161
the attention of people
working on services,

00:34:40.161 --> 00:34:42.955
regardless to the type of
customers or the persona

00:34:42.955 --> 00:34:46.042
of the customer, we need
to focus on that experience

00:34:46.042 --> 00:34:47.210
of that customer.

00:34:47.210 --> 00:34:50.129
Now who is part of the GX, everyone.

00:34:50.129 --> 00:34:53.591
So it could be private
sector, could be investors,

00:34:53.591 --> 00:34:55.968
startups, entrepreneurs.

00:34:55.968 --> 00:34:58.179
Who could be part of the GX platform,

00:34:58.179 --> 00:34:59.847
has multiple aspects to it,

00:34:59.847 --> 00:35:03.184
we have the government services
forum that brings leaders

00:35:03.184 --> 00:35:05.770
from around the world,
around the table to discuss

00:35:05.770 --> 00:35:07.355
government services.

00:35:07.355 --> 00:35:10.858
We have the GX online platform
that shares knowledge base,

00:35:10.858 --> 00:35:12.360
how governments around the world

00:35:12.360 --> 00:35:14.695
improve customer experience.

00:35:14.695 --> 00:35:16.989
We have also the GX talks.

00:35:16.989 --> 00:35:20.785
So the GX talks also focused
on getting thought leaders

00:35:20.785 --> 00:35:23.746
from different backgrounds
to talk about experience

00:35:23.746 --> 00:35:26.249
of customers, whether
it is digital experience

00:35:26.249 --> 00:35:28.709
or it is also the physical experience

00:35:28.709 --> 00:35:31.879
or even experience that
has no touch point.

00:35:31.879 --> 00:35:34.382
So for example, if you sit at home,

00:35:34.382 --> 00:35:38.678
how is your feeling about the
you get from the government?

00:35:38.678 --> 00:35:41.681
Are you worried about
missing out or forgetting

00:35:41.681 --> 00:35:44.142
about paying your bills,
your utilities bills

00:35:44.142 --> 00:35:45.977
or are you comfortable because you know

00:35:45.977 --> 00:35:48.855
that it will automatically be processed.

00:35:48.855 --> 00:35:50.398
- The UAE is an impressive nation.

00:35:50.398 --> 00:35:52.942
They've got the advantage
of being fast and nimble

00:35:52.942 --> 00:35:54.986
and agile right now in the way

00:35:54.986 --> 00:35:57.071
that they go after initiatives.

00:35:57.071 --> 00:36:01.200
Plus they've got leadership
that's truly vested and believes

00:36:01.200 --> 00:36:03.286
in going digital and adopting

00:36:03.286 --> 00:36:04.620
the fourth industrial revolution.

00:36:04.620 --> 00:36:07.665
I think these ingredients,
when you put them together

00:36:07.665 --> 00:36:12.295
gives you a very unique
environment because of the platform

00:36:12.295 --> 00:36:14.755
that's been created by investing

00:36:14.755 --> 00:36:16.424
in this futuristic thinking.

00:36:16.424 --> 00:36:18.885
- It's not just their
vision and their talk,

00:36:19.844 --> 00:36:22.180
but they're building
things and doing things.

00:36:22.180 --> 00:36:24.599
I've been able to experience
these things firsthand

00:36:24.599 --> 00:36:25.433
in Dubai

00:36:33.357 --> 00:36:37.195
For example, their concept
around the future police station.

00:36:37.195 --> 00:36:38.654
It's a beautiful experience.

00:36:38.654 --> 00:36:40.656
People feel safe.

00:36:40.656 --> 00:36:43.159
And a lot of it is done by
use of digital technologies,

00:36:43.159 --> 00:36:47.205
assistance from the right
resources via video conference,

00:36:47.205 --> 00:36:49.040
which I thought was incredible.

00:36:49.040 --> 00:36:50.958
You see a lot of
experimentation and prototyping,

00:36:50.958 --> 00:36:54.837
you see very bold projects,
everything from smart Dubai

00:36:54.837 --> 00:36:58.591
right through to the paperless
society, thus making the cost

00:36:58.591 --> 00:37:00.927
of government be less,
data to be more accurate

00:37:00.927 --> 00:37:02.470
all the benefits we would expect.

00:37:02.470 --> 00:37:05.848
One of the big ones the
environmental positive side of not

00:37:05.848 --> 00:37:06.682
having oldest paper.

00:37:06.682 --> 00:37:08.809
They're doing the things,
they're demonstrating,

00:37:08.809 --> 00:37:10.770
they're experimenting, they're
helping other communities

00:37:10.770 --> 00:37:13.064
and cities and governments
around the world

00:37:13.064 --> 00:37:13.898
see what's possible.

00:37:13.898 --> 00:37:15.691
Sharing these insights with the world,

00:37:15.691 --> 00:37:18.277
I'm wanting you to create
things like the GX forum,

00:37:18.277 --> 00:37:19.487
which becomes a global platform to say,

00:37:19.487 --> 00:37:21.614
hey what there's different ways

00:37:21.614 --> 00:37:23.282
to have a government experience.

00:37:23.282 --> 00:37:26.118
We've got to be able to
create channels that takes

00:37:26.118 --> 00:37:28.246
these best practices,
the stuff that goes well

00:37:28.246 --> 00:37:30.122
and maybe even the learnings of the stuff

00:37:30.122 --> 00:37:32.124
that doesn't go so well all the time

00:37:32.124 --> 00:37:35.294
and share it with governments
in every corner of the world.

00:37:35.294 --> 00:37:37.713
- So I think the thing that's
important about it being

00:37:37.713 --> 00:37:39.257
an international conversation
and frankly being

00:37:39.257 --> 00:37:42.468
in-person conversation,
because we're all busy.

00:37:42.468 --> 00:37:46.430
If we just try to get
together by phone or webinar,

00:37:46.430 --> 00:37:48.099
we're gonna get distracted though.

00:37:48.099 --> 00:37:51.811
But when you plant yourself
physically with other people

00:37:51.811 --> 00:37:55.439
you make the kinds of
connections that last

00:37:55.439 --> 00:37:58.818
and that allow us to
keep advancing the ball

00:37:58.818 --> 00:38:02.697
to continue to refine our thinking

00:38:02.697 --> 00:38:06.158
and to challenge each
other that sense of almost

00:38:06.158 --> 00:38:09.745
like a healthy peer competition,
like, oh I'm gonna have

00:38:10.579 --> 00:38:12.081
to have something to say
when I get back together

00:38:12.081 --> 00:38:13.207
with this group, what have I done?

00:38:13.207 --> 00:38:15.334
What do I bring to the table?

00:38:15.334 --> 00:38:16.585
So I think that's a real value

00:38:16.585 --> 00:38:18.004
that we can see in this group.

00:38:18.004 --> 00:38:20.715
- It's really necessary
to have experiments

00:38:20.715 --> 00:38:23.467
within your own organization,
but otherwise it's still

00:38:23.467 --> 00:38:26.304
complicated to have these
discussions with other partners

00:38:26.304 --> 00:38:27.680
who develop services.

00:38:27.680 --> 00:38:29.807
So you must test and explore a little bit

00:38:29.807 --> 00:38:31.392
within your own organization.

00:38:31.392 --> 00:38:33.519
You must train your employees,

00:38:33.519 --> 00:38:35.187
there are hardly any projects,

00:38:35.187 --> 00:38:36.939
very don't have to collaborate.

00:38:36.939 --> 00:38:40.234
So it's really important
to learn how you can build

00:38:40.234 --> 00:38:44.322
from coalitions and I've
kind of agreements we need,

00:38:44.322 --> 00:38:46.699
the legal issues around
these collaborations,

00:38:46.699 --> 00:38:47.825
the governance models.

00:38:47.825 --> 00:38:51.245
- Of course, it is very
difficult to implement our ideas

00:38:51.245 --> 00:38:52.246
as it is.

00:38:52.246 --> 00:38:56.334
Because every country has own
cultures, as on tradition,

00:38:56.334 --> 00:38:57.626
as on environment.

00:38:57.626 --> 00:39:01.714
And you can see how they are
challenged in that environment

00:39:01.714 --> 00:39:04.216
and how they solve that problems

00:39:04.216 --> 00:39:05.968
during the implementation process.

00:39:05.968 --> 00:39:07.803
- Initiatives like this can often tend

00:39:07.803 --> 00:39:11.390
to be wanting to come up with
the best global solution.

00:39:11.390 --> 00:39:14.185
But these ideas are
evolving, the relationship

00:39:14.185 --> 00:39:16.187
with citizens states is evolving.

00:39:16.187 --> 00:39:17.772
Technology is evolving.

00:39:17.772 --> 00:39:20.816
So a little bit more
I think self-critique,

00:39:20.816 --> 00:39:24.487
and not always positive
about some of the activities

00:39:24.487 --> 00:39:26.822
that happen, because
technology can use for good

00:39:26.822 --> 00:39:28.407
and it can be used for bad.

00:39:28.407 --> 00:39:33.412
And it's how immediate those
in the interests of citizens.

00:39:33.496 --> 00:39:35.373
For me citizens are
actually more important

00:39:35.373 --> 00:39:36.916
than governments.

00:39:36.916 --> 00:39:38.751
Governments should serve their citizens.

00:39:51.055 --> 00:39:53.766
- We have learned for
me residency experiences

00:39:53.766 --> 00:39:57.728
to try to put yourself
in the shoes of the user.

00:39:57.728 --> 00:39:59.480
Because we see quite often that that's

00:39:59.480 --> 00:40:01.315
what government officials are lacking.

00:40:01.315 --> 00:40:05.069
So they sit in their silos and
they don't talk to the users,

00:40:05.069 --> 00:40:08.239
they don't understand the
needs of the users in the age

00:40:08.239 --> 00:40:11.534
of smartphones and all of
the private sector services

00:40:11.534 --> 00:40:14.537
that we actually consume all the time.

00:40:14.537 --> 00:40:18.207
We can't expect our
governments to lag behind.

00:40:18.207 --> 00:40:20.668
We believe in Estonia that
governments can never compete

00:40:20.668 --> 00:40:22.920
with private companies, and they should,

00:40:22.920 --> 00:40:25.047
but governments are the ones

00:40:25.047 --> 00:40:26.048
who have to offer government services.

00:40:26.048 --> 00:40:28.843
So it's time to actually go together

00:40:28.843 --> 00:40:31.470
with the trends what's
happening in the world.

00:40:34.515 --> 00:40:37.893
The global star rating program,
is the first of its kind

00:40:37.893 --> 00:40:40.688
that assesses government service centers

00:40:40.688 --> 00:40:42.398
as if they are cartels.

00:40:42.398 --> 00:40:44.483
So just like when you walk into a hotel

00:40:44.483 --> 00:40:47.027
and you see that this
hotel is a five-star hotel

00:40:47.027 --> 00:40:49.864
and you understand what
experience you're gonna be getting

00:40:49.864 --> 00:40:51.198
from a five-star hotel,

00:40:51.198 --> 00:40:53.117
the same similar concept has been applied

00:40:53.117 --> 00:40:54.827
to government service centers.

00:40:54.827 --> 00:40:59.832
Hassan Chef Hamad, guided us
to make United Arab Emirates

00:40:59.874 --> 00:41:03.878
government as efficient
as banks and works 24/7

00:41:03.878 --> 00:41:04.837
like airports.

00:41:04.837 --> 00:41:08.257
Since this program has been
launched, we have realized

00:41:08.257 --> 00:41:10.926
the fruits of this program by seeing

00:41:10.926 --> 00:41:13.471
customer happiness center
has improved significantly

00:41:13.471 --> 00:41:14.930
over the past eight years.

00:41:14.930 --> 00:41:17.683
- The Edelman Trust Barometer
ranks the UAE very high

00:41:17.683 --> 00:41:19.226
the highest it's not the
second highest in the region

00:41:19.226 --> 00:41:20.603
and one of the highest in the world,

00:41:20.603 --> 00:41:22.021
I think with the focus centers.

00:41:22.021 --> 00:41:24.648
The UAE has always designed
its government services

00:41:24.648 --> 00:41:27.735
and looked at government
services as actually an enabler

00:41:27.735 --> 00:41:28.569
to society.

00:41:28.569 --> 00:41:29.862
Today the UAE is looking forward and say,

00:41:29.862 --> 00:41:31.989
I want to do even more than even better.

00:41:31.989 --> 00:41:33.699
I want to be even more customer centric.

00:41:33.699 --> 00:41:36.619
I want to make sure that
my government services

00:41:36.619 --> 00:41:37.745
are actually not just
the best in the world,

00:41:37.745 --> 00:41:39.955
but the most satisfactory
in the world for the people

00:41:39.955 --> 00:41:40.789
that choose them.

00:41:40.789 --> 00:41:42.750
And actually, how can
I enhance their life?

00:41:42.750 --> 00:41:45.586
How can I actually add
value to their lives?

00:41:45.586 --> 00:41:48.005
And this is something that
we're seeing happening day in

00:41:48.005 --> 00:41:49.298
day out.

00:41:49.298 --> 00:41:50.799
This culture of transparency
that's coming across more

00:41:50.799 --> 00:41:52.176
and more in the UAE.

00:41:52.176 --> 00:41:53.344
In order to be the best in the world,

00:41:53.344 --> 00:41:54.970
we have also to hold up the mirror.

00:41:54.970 --> 00:41:56.639
And this is something that
you're seeing happening

00:41:56.639 --> 00:41:58.766
and that think the government
is leading by example.

00:42:14.156 --> 00:42:16.617
- The future of the GX program

00:42:16.617 --> 00:42:20.120
by the prime minister's office
in the United Arab Emirates

00:42:20.120 --> 00:42:21.205
is strong.

00:42:21.205 --> 00:42:22.998
It's something that's tangible.

00:42:22.998 --> 00:42:26.126
It's something that can
be developed and adopted

00:42:26.126 --> 00:42:29.922
by every government service
program in the world.

00:42:29.922 --> 00:42:33.008
But it needs work, it needs
work, because implementation

00:42:33.008 --> 00:42:34.843
of these things is complex.

00:42:34.843 --> 00:42:37.846
You need stakeholders to
agree on these things.

00:42:37.846 --> 00:42:41.725
You need to get the buy-in from
people who run governments.

00:42:41.725 --> 00:42:44.728
And those are some of the
challenges that other governments

00:42:44.728 --> 00:42:47.439
might face when adopting this program.

00:42:47.439 --> 00:42:52.278
We live in an era of disruption,
fast changing expectations

00:42:52.278 --> 00:42:55.072
in order to make the world a better place,

00:42:55.072 --> 00:42:58.492
we will have to play the
game in a different way

00:42:58.492 --> 00:43:01.662
whether it's GX or any other program

00:43:01.662 --> 00:43:03.414
at a government service level,

00:43:03.414 --> 00:43:06.292
it needs the commitment of the leadership

00:43:06.292 --> 00:43:09.211
of these countries, of these
departments and groups.

00:43:09.211 --> 00:43:10.879
- So this is an amazing time.

00:43:10.879 --> 00:43:14.383
There is so much potential
and we need two things.

00:43:14.383 --> 00:43:17.344
We need, number one, to
listen and number two to act.

00:43:17.344 --> 00:43:19.263
And when you put those two things together

00:43:19.263 --> 00:43:20.848
it makes a huge difference.

00:43:20.848 --> 00:43:23.601
So in the US the veterans administration

00:43:23.601 --> 00:43:25.853
which serves former
members of the military

00:43:25.853 --> 00:43:27.688
who are now back in the country,

00:43:27.688 --> 00:43:31.942
they started a customer
satisfaction survey,

00:43:31.942 --> 00:43:34.903
and it turns out people
who responded to the survey

00:43:34.903 --> 00:43:38.616
and gave feedback were
four times more satisfied

00:43:38.616 --> 00:43:39.867
with government than others.

00:43:39.867 --> 00:43:43.787
Now don't know yet whether
some of that is a sample bias,

00:43:43.787 --> 00:43:46.373
that those who decided
to answer the survey

00:43:46.373 --> 00:43:47.249
were more satisfied.

00:43:47.249 --> 00:43:50.919
But to me, it also says that
there is power in just asking

00:43:50.919 --> 00:43:51.754
for feedback.

00:43:51.754 --> 00:43:55.382
When you engage the public,
they become more excited.

00:43:55.382 --> 00:43:58.218
The city of Santa Monica,
California has a wellbeing index.

00:43:58.218 --> 00:44:01.305
They have decided that
the purpose of that city

00:44:01.305 --> 00:44:04.058
is to support the wellbeing of its people.

00:44:04.058 --> 00:44:06.310
One of the things they've done

00:44:06.310 --> 00:44:08.312
is social media sentiment mining.

00:44:08.312 --> 00:44:11.106
So they can tell which
neighborhoods are showing

00:44:11.106 --> 00:44:13.651
through the publicly available information

00:44:13.651 --> 00:44:14.985
on Facebook and Twitter.

00:44:14.985 --> 00:44:17.655
What neighborhoods are under stress,

00:44:17.655 --> 00:44:19.323
what neighborhoods are worried about money

00:44:19.323 --> 00:44:22.159
and how can the city really
do something about that.

00:44:22.159 --> 00:44:23.619
We now have drug treatment in jails

00:44:23.619 --> 00:44:25.996
because the people who were coming out

00:44:25.996 --> 00:44:28.957
of jails were 120 times more likely

00:44:28.957 --> 00:44:31.585
than the average person
to die of drug overdose.

00:44:31.585 --> 00:44:34.588
So now we can do
interventions in our jails,

00:44:34.588 --> 00:44:37.049
because the data approach
allowed us to target.

00:44:37.049 --> 00:44:39.927
When community members got to participate

00:44:39.927 --> 00:44:44.098
and help the government
choose where the money goes.

00:44:44.098 --> 00:44:45.724
When the people made the decisions

00:44:45.724 --> 00:44:47.351
there was more investment in health,

00:44:47.351 --> 00:44:49.019
more investment in education.

00:44:49.019 --> 00:44:51.480
And some of those early
cities that employed this,

00:44:51.480 --> 00:44:54.942
showed lower incidents of infant mortality

00:44:54.942 --> 00:44:57.069
five years, 10 years from
now, I think we're gonna have

00:44:57.069 --> 00:45:01.031
a much higher level of trust
in government, because with all

00:45:01.031 --> 00:45:03.283
of these new ways of engaging the public,

00:45:03.283 --> 00:45:07.663
there are untapped ideas and resources

00:45:07.663 --> 00:45:12.668
and potential for really making
government just far better

00:45:14.044 --> 00:45:15.295
than it's ever been.

00:45:15.295 --> 00:45:18.465
- We are planning to implement
personalized services

00:45:18.465 --> 00:45:22.469
for our citizens, from any
country, from any place,

00:45:22.469 --> 00:45:24.263
from the result and barriers.

00:45:24.263 --> 00:45:28.058
And to be accessible and
available for that we need also

00:45:28.058 --> 00:45:32.771
to implement a new technologies,
AI machine learning,

00:45:32.771 --> 00:45:34.732
deep learning and other technologies.

00:45:34.732 --> 00:45:37.317
Now we are implemented
shared cloud-based document

00:45:37.317 --> 00:45:40.946
floor system and it helps
us to reduce the costs

00:45:40.946 --> 00:45:44.950
for building that services and
to optimize that processes.

00:45:57.504 --> 00:46:00.591
- The governments of the future
are going to be focused more

00:46:00.591 --> 00:46:02.092
on purpose and less on services.

00:46:02.092 --> 00:46:03.552
Services are gonna be done.

00:46:03.552 --> 00:46:05.512
Artificial intelligence
machine learning is gonna

00:46:05.512 --> 00:46:07.931
take care of most of the
services being delivered.

00:46:07.931 --> 00:46:10.476
We're gonna be focusing more
on thinking about our future

00:46:10.476 --> 00:46:13.896
on how we can magnify life
as a nation, as a community

00:46:13.896 --> 00:46:16.774
the governments will have
much more time capabilities

00:46:16.774 --> 00:46:19.818
and much higher caution when
it gets to the livability,

00:46:19.818 --> 00:46:23.280
when it gets to the future of
government is gonna be about.

00:46:23.280 --> 00:46:25.324
A lot of the problems that we know today,

00:46:25.324 --> 00:46:27.159
a lot of the topics that
you are trying to solve

00:46:27.159 --> 00:46:29.161
for today are not gonna
be there in 10 years time.

00:46:29.161 --> 00:46:31.497
We are all gonna be
augmented in our capabilities

00:46:31.497 --> 00:46:32.331
as human beings.

00:46:32.331 --> 00:46:33.665
We're gonna be able to do more for less,

00:46:33.665 --> 00:46:37.336
but also we're gonna be able
to actually focus on where

00:46:37.336 --> 00:46:39.296
we actually make the
biggest difference and drive

00:46:39.296 --> 00:46:42.883
the biggest impact, not in
terms of the small things

00:46:42.883 --> 00:46:44.927
that happen around us, in terms
of enriching life on earth

00:46:44.927 --> 00:46:48.222
and how we can actually be
a continuous agent of change

00:46:48.222 --> 00:46:49.056
and progress.

00:46:51.767 --> 00:46:56.480
- I believe that AI is definitely
going to be the next cycle

00:46:56.480 --> 00:46:59.107
of major revolution in
technology and what we're gonna

00:46:59.107 --> 00:47:01.652
be able to do with it
are a lot of products

00:47:01.652 --> 00:47:05.072
and services that were
not possible in the past.

00:47:05.072 --> 00:47:07.866
But it's not my belief that
the AI algorithm is gonna

00:47:07.866 --> 00:47:10.911
become sentient one day and
wake up, then figure out

00:47:10.911 --> 00:47:12.621
that humans need to be eradicated.

00:47:12.621 --> 00:47:15.582
Let's be more pragmatic and
more realistic, and let's not

00:47:15.582 --> 00:47:16.917
worry about the scifi part.

00:47:16.917 --> 00:47:21.129
The part of AI that's really
exciting are most helpful

00:47:21.129 --> 00:47:24.132
for corporate user or
the corporate worker.

00:47:24.132 --> 00:47:26.176
The first one is this
concept of digital twin.

00:47:26.176 --> 00:47:28.303
And the concept of the digital twin,

00:47:28.303 --> 00:47:30.138
is that it's an assistant
working side-by-side

00:47:30.138 --> 00:47:33.308
with the human to be able
to improve the quality

00:47:33.308 --> 00:47:36.478
of the type of work that
they're doing by relegating

00:47:36.478 --> 00:47:40.023
all the mundane manual
tasks over to the AI

00:47:40.023 --> 00:47:41.358
and the automation.

00:47:41.358 --> 00:47:44.027
So that the human is actually focusing

00:47:44.027 --> 00:47:44.862
on the things that they
should be focusing on,

00:47:44.862 --> 00:47:47.739
not filing the papers, not
thumping through things

00:47:47.739 --> 00:47:51.952
and trying to index, 1000
pages that should all be done

00:47:51.952 --> 00:47:52.786
by machines.

00:47:52.786 --> 00:47:55.581
And the digital twin
should be providing this

00:47:55.581 --> 00:47:58.959
workflow automation for the
human to become more effective

00:47:58.959 --> 00:48:00.544
and more efficient.

00:48:00.544 --> 00:48:03.881
- A lot of services
will be global services.

00:48:03.881 --> 00:48:06.425
And then on top of that or beside that

00:48:06.425 --> 00:48:10.637
you will have a local flavor
but what I see, and for example

00:48:10.637 --> 00:48:13.390
it's the blockchain projects,
the registration of diplomas,

00:48:13.390 --> 00:48:15.851
now it's done by countries by themselves.

00:48:15.851 --> 00:48:18.020
I expect this will be a global surface.

00:48:18.020 --> 00:48:20.689
We bring people from all these coalitions

00:48:20.689 --> 00:48:23.150
and all these communities
together to work on these

00:48:23.150 --> 00:48:26.403
challenges, which are
mainly global challenges.

00:48:26.403 --> 00:48:29.448
And I think at this moment,
the first step to bring

00:48:29.448 --> 00:48:32.618
these experts together,
they need each other,

00:48:32.618 --> 00:48:33.452
for example, the blockchain experts

00:48:33.452 --> 00:48:36.538
they need more and more data,
and so they need the experts

00:48:36.538 --> 00:48:39.207
from the big data working
group, but also the data experts

00:48:39.207 --> 00:48:41.668
from the artificial
intelligence working group.

00:48:41.668 --> 00:48:44.755
So the next step is to
build brother collaborations

00:48:44.755 --> 00:48:45.589
worldwide.

00:48:45.589 --> 00:48:47.674
And I hope the Netherlands
can be the gateway

00:48:47.674 --> 00:48:50.135
to Europe for many services.

00:48:50.135 --> 00:48:52.638
- The future of government
services should be something

00:48:52.638 --> 00:48:55.390
that happens without the
customer asking for it.

00:48:55.390 --> 00:48:58.852
We know what the customers want,
why not deliver it to them?

00:48:58.852 --> 00:49:02.147
Why not go to the customer,
instead of having a customer

00:49:02.147 --> 00:49:04.358
come to us, we want to relate it more

00:49:04.358 --> 00:49:07.986
to the digital experience
that customers today have

00:49:07.986 --> 00:49:11.114
with online shopping, where
they get recommendations

00:49:11.114 --> 00:49:14.826
on what to buy based on their
previous searches on the time

00:49:14.826 --> 00:49:18.330
of the year, we want to be
something similar to that

00:49:18.330 --> 00:49:20.248
and maybe even more proactive.

00:49:20.248 --> 00:49:22.459
We are aware that the
way education is going to

00:49:22.459 --> 00:49:24.211
be delivered is going to change.

00:49:24.211 --> 00:49:27.005
We are aware that healthcare
is going to change,

00:49:27.005 --> 00:49:30.342
all of these services need
to be integrated in a way

00:49:30.342 --> 00:49:33.095
and in a manner that makes it seamless

00:49:33.095 --> 00:49:36.390
to the customer is to the
investor of the future.

00:49:36.390 --> 00:49:39.851
- We have taken most of
our services online already

00:49:39.851 --> 00:49:43.438
but now it's the matter of
actually measuring the quality

00:49:43.438 --> 00:49:44.606
of the services.

00:49:44.606 --> 00:49:46.483
How do they function?

00:49:46.483 --> 00:49:47.651
What could be improved?

00:49:47.651 --> 00:49:49.736
What's the journey behind,

00:49:49.736 --> 00:49:51.571
you can do most of the stuff online,

00:49:51.571 --> 00:49:54.616
but we still have lots
of relic in the process.

00:49:54.616 --> 00:49:58.829
In Estonia when we opened up
our services to non citizens

00:49:58.829 --> 00:50:03.583
using the e-Residency, that
means that we might have

00:50:03.583 --> 00:50:05.293
some Estonian documents
from time to time pop up

00:50:05.293 --> 00:50:07.462
to the foreigners in our systems.

00:50:07.462 --> 00:50:09.923
That's something that needs to be worked.

00:50:09.923 --> 00:50:13.468
We work in towards the
perception of having this

00:50:13.468 --> 00:50:15.595
seamless background services.

00:50:15.595 --> 00:50:17.514
Once you are born, we
already know your name.

00:50:17.514 --> 00:50:18.849
We know your birthdate.

00:50:18.849 --> 00:50:20.475
We know quite a lot of things about you.

00:50:20.475 --> 00:50:21.935
Same for medicine, for example.

00:50:21.935 --> 00:50:23.729
We're looking at some
of the ways where we can

00:50:23.729 --> 00:50:28.525
predict what illnesses you
might have or genetic about you

00:50:28.525 --> 00:50:29.901
and the like.

00:50:30.986 --> 00:50:31.987
- We've been very pragmatic
in a sense to say, look

00:50:31.987 --> 00:50:34.948
let's figure out how to solve
this organizational illegally

00:50:34.948 --> 00:50:37.284
have proper safeguards in
place technology as well.

00:50:37.284 --> 00:50:39.369
So this sort of approach
to fixing problems

00:50:39.369 --> 00:50:41.121
and sort of solving them.

00:50:41.121 --> 00:50:43.290
And it's a very engineer
way, even through design

00:50:43.290 --> 00:50:44.207
is one part of it.

00:50:44.207 --> 00:50:47.169
And the other part we have
benefited from the fact

00:50:47.169 --> 00:50:49.838
that obviously we are small
country in that sense.

00:50:49.838 --> 00:50:52.382
And the size doesn't matter
really for the point of view

00:50:52.382 --> 00:50:55.052
how many people that live,
but just the, I would say

00:50:55.052 --> 00:50:58.013
that as a small country, we
have very lean governance

00:50:58.013 --> 00:51:00.057
in terms of how decisions are made.

00:51:00.057 --> 00:51:02.726
And I think if oftentimes,
if countries asking me, okay

00:51:02.726 --> 00:51:04.853
so what makes us don't a bit more nimble,

00:51:04.853 --> 00:51:07.272
because decision-making is
faster and not too many levels

00:51:07.272 --> 00:51:09.816
of government to consult and so forth.

00:51:09.816 --> 00:51:12.611
So that's what has allowed
us to go quite a bit way.

00:51:13.612 --> 00:51:14.488
And I've seen in bigger countries too,

00:51:14.488 --> 00:51:17.157
if some decision-making
gets streamlined, of course

00:51:17.157 --> 00:51:20.035
in democratic context, then
you're able to make leaps.

00:51:20.035 --> 00:51:22.037
Because tech moves fast, you
have to be able to move faster

00:51:22.037 --> 00:51:22.871
along as well.

00:51:32.881 --> 00:51:34.549
- What were really change the world,

00:51:34.549 --> 00:51:37.594
is when enlightened
progressive governments

00:51:37.594 --> 00:51:42.599
are able to easily connect with
enlightened active citizens,

00:51:42.724 --> 00:51:43.975
because that's a potent force.

00:51:43.975 --> 00:51:46.103
You bring those two things
together and we can do

00:51:46.103 --> 00:51:47.604
some really amazing things.

00:51:47.604 --> 00:51:51.316
This sort of tension that
exists between communities

00:51:51.316 --> 00:51:54.653
and governments, because
communities have great expectation

00:51:54.653 --> 00:51:57.405
that governments try hard,
but they struggle to meet

00:51:57.405 --> 00:51:58.406
those expectations.

00:51:58.406 --> 00:52:01.243
In a digital world you can
deliver more consistently

00:52:01.243 --> 00:52:03.995
and getting access to information and data

00:52:03.995 --> 00:52:04.830
when they need it,

00:52:04.830 --> 00:52:07.999
creating more of a seamless
channel between when the data

00:52:07.999 --> 00:52:10.043
is created by government
or collected by government

00:52:10.043 --> 00:52:12.462
and the desire to be access that,

00:52:12.462 --> 00:52:14.673
simply because we wanna know
what government is collecting

00:52:14.673 --> 00:52:17.509
on us, but also to be able
to make better decisions

00:52:17.509 --> 00:52:19.469
and to develop solutions.

00:52:25.433 --> 00:52:27.978
There will be a much
greater degree of automation

00:52:27.978 --> 00:52:28.812
in the future.

00:52:28.812 --> 00:52:31.731
And this will be both
in the digital world,

00:52:31.731 --> 00:52:34.693
so the use of artificial intelligence,

00:52:34.693 --> 00:52:36.361
but also through robotics.

00:52:36.361 --> 00:52:38.071
And so we will have questions about,

00:52:38.071 --> 00:52:40.365
what does that mean to
the human experience?

00:52:40.365 --> 00:52:42.826
What does that mean to jobs?

00:52:42.826 --> 00:52:45.495
What does that mean to the
personal connection we have

00:52:45.495 --> 00:52:47.622
between an individual and a provider?

00:52:47.622 --> 00:52:49.040
So we'll see all the positives

00:52:49.040 --> 00:52:51.668
of automation and artificial intelligence.

00:52:51.668 --> 00:52:53.920
We also gotta be conscious
of what that means

00:52:53.920 --> 00:52:56.840
ultimately to us as humans.

00:52:56.840 --> 00:52:59.134
We have to decide what
we want to do about that.

00:52:59.134 --> 00:53:02.512
There'll be more change
in the next 10 to 20 years

00:53:02.512 --> 00:53:04.139
than in the last 100 years,

00:53:04.139 --> 00:53:05.599
when it comes to government experience

00:53:05.599 --> 00:53:07.225
it's gonna be a remarkable time.

00:53:10.770 --> 00:53:13.023
- All the different
methodologies available today

00:53:13.023 --> 00:53:15.734
including the GX program are amazing.

00:53:15.734 --> 00:53:18.111
They can change how citizens are served.

00:53:18.111 --> 00:53:22.699
They can change how citizens
you and I access services

00:53:22.699 --> 00:53:25.744
at a government level in
the country of our choice.

00:53:25.744 --> 00:53:29.789
I really believe governments
need to look at GX

00:53:29.789 --> 00:53:33.627
as a program that can
bring a new wave of energy,

00:53:33.627 --> 00:53:36.338
that can bring efficiency
and accountability

00:53:36.338 --> 00:53:37.380
in their government.

00:53:41.968 --> 00:53:45.430
- The United Nations predicts
that global population

00:53:45.430 --> 00:53:49.100
will reach 9.8 billion people by 2050.

00:53:49.100 --> 00:53:51.728
This means more expectations
from governments

00:53:51.728 --> 00:53:55.232
to serve citizens better and
create better experiences.

00:53:55.232 --> 00:53:58.985
This means a more complex
world with new stakeholders,

00:53:58.985 --> 00:54:02.405
new challenges, and
new speeds of response.

00:54:02.405 --> 00:54:07.369
In 2020 humanity battle, a new
challenge, COVID 19 pandemic

00:54:07.994 --> 00:54:10.664
many government struggled
to cope with the pandemic

00:54:10.664 --> 00:54:14.834
and yet many succeeded beyond
expectations in managing,

00:54:14.834 --> 00:54:18.546
controlling and eliminating
the threat of the pandemic.

00:54:18.546 --> 00:54:21.925
One thing all responsive
and successful governments

00:54:21.925 --> 00:54:25.136
did was their ability to respond fast,

00:54:25.136 --> 00:54:28.765
to engage with citizens
and to focus on success

00:54:28.765 --> 00:54:31.685
while looking to the future.

00:54:31.685 --> 00:54:36.523
It is these governments,
thinkers, leaders and trailblazers

00:54:36.523 --> 00:54:40.193
that are in the process of
creating a better world.

00:54:56.293 --> 00:54:58.169
- If you believe that
there is a social contract

00:54:58.169 --> 00:55:00.505
between the governed and the government,

00:55:00.505 --> 00:55:03.758
then the things that
governments need to keep in mind

00:55:04.634 --> 00:55:06.970
when they provide services is,

00:55:06.970 --> 00:55:10.473
what people really want to
get from that relationship.

00:55:13.643 --> 00:55:16.271
- Sometimes the solution that will work

00:55:16.271 --> 00:55:17.397
in one place won't work in another.

00:55:17.397 --> 00:55:21.192
So being very curious about
the histories and cultures

00:55:21.192 --> 00:55:23.903
in which you're working
in order to match up

00:55:23.903 --> 00:55:27.991
the services with the particular residents

00:55:27.991 --> 00:55:29.826
or citizens that they're serving,

00:55:29.826 --> 00:55:31.578
I think is a real leadership skill.

00:55:35.957 --> 00:55:39.127
- Making it an outside in
approach to services delivery

00:55:39.127 --> 00:55:41.379
as opposed to inside the government out,

00:55:41.379 --> 00:55:44.883
is a change of a paradigm of
how government traditionally

00:55:44.883 --> 00:55:47.302
develops and delivers
policy, and then thinks

00:55:47.302 --> 00:55:48.428
about service delivery.

00:55:51.139 --> 00:55:54.267
- Challenges that governments
face are no longer technical

00:55:54.267 --> 00:55:57.103
in nature, those we
figured out how to solve.

00:55:57.103 --> 00:56:00.523
The problems that are facing
governments today are more

00:56:00.523 --> 00:56:01.691
around wicked problems,

00:56:01.691 --> 00:56:03.735
such as how do we solve homelessness?

00:56:04.569 --> 00:56:07.655
How do we improve food delivery to people?

00:56:07.655 --> 00:56:09.657
How do we improve
people's quality of life?

00:56:09.657 --> 00:56:12.077
- We started with public policy
focusing a lot on markets

00:56:12.077 --> 00:56:15.163
and market failure and how
government had to solve problems

00:56:15.163 --> 00:56:15.997
with market failure.

00:56:15.997 --> 00:56:17.749
I think that's still an important
thing, but I think we have

00:56:17.749 --> 00:56:19.709
to go beyond that now and
understand that equity

00:56:19.709 --> 00:56:22.170
and justice are really
important factors that we have

00:56:22.170 --> 00:56:24.422
to take into account and
figure out how we can devise

00:56:24.422 --> 00:56:25.882
public policies that make sure

00:56:25.882 --> 00:56:28.385
that we're being equitable
and we're being fair and just.

00:56:28.385 --> 00:56:29.677
- There are places that are excelling

00:56:29.677 --> 00:56:32.806
in doing digital services,
but I have yet to see

00:56:32.806 --> 00:56:36.184
a government that then harvest
the data on the backend

00:56:36.184 --> 00:56:39.354
for customer insight,
for predictive analytics,

00:56:39.354 --> 00:56:42.607
geospatial analysis and
really optimizing performance.

00:56:42.607 --> 00:56:45.235
- Just because you have a
large, large dataset does not

00:56:45.235 --> 00:56:47.695
necessarily mean it can
help you in any way,

00:56:47.695 --> 00:56:50.824
if you can't unlock value or
information from that raw data.

00:56:50.824 --> 00:56:53.201
And so the way I think about
how government can play a role

00:56:53.201 --> 00:56:55.829
is to really focus on
information that's valuable

00:56:55.829 --> 00:56:59.374
for constituents, for
policy makers, for citizens,

00:56:59.374 --> 00:57:03.044
for residents, or for
visitors, use that information

00:57:03.044 --> 00:57:05.922
to all be on the same page
for what success looks like

00:57:05.922 --> 00:57:08.716
and how to assess whether
or not you've hit criteria

00:57:08.716 --> 00:57:11.428
that you've set and then ultimately learn

00:57:11.428 --> 00:57:12.846
and iterate from there.

00:57:20.270 --> 00:57:22.105
- I think customers have expectations

00:57:22.105 --> 00:57:25.108
that are drastically different
than what governments

00:57:25.108 --> 00:57:26.985
offered today and services.

00:57:26.985 --> 00:57:28.486
And we should bridge the gap.

00:57:28.486 --> 00:57:30.822
- I worry that in a downturn,
we're gonna have to be even

00:57:30.822 --> 00:57:32.824
more efficient in the way we serve.

00:57:32.824 --> 00:57:36.995
And with the expansion of digital
services, we need to think

00:57:36.995 --> 00:57:39.914
about how we do old fashioned
services in a new way.

00:57:39.914 --> 00:57:42.959
- One thing that we do within
a city can impact traffic,

00:57:42.959 --> 00:57:44.752
which can impact housing,

00:57:44.752 --> 00:57:48.256
which can impact the migration of people.

00:57:48.256 --> 00:57:49.966
We have to consider everything

00:57:49.966 --> 00:57:54.137
within systems and within
the greater mechanisms

00:57:54.137 --> 00:57:56.806
of this and not just
on individual decisions

00:57:56.806 --> 00:57:58.349
- Make sure you have
the right business cases

00:57:58.349 --> 00:58:01.102
and the right governance in
place that you can't innovate

00:58:01.102 --> 00:58:03.146
if it takes a year to
approve a business case

00:58:03.146 --> 00:58:04.314
to do a new service.

00:58:15.700 --> 00:58:18.203
- The one thing that government
needs to focus on primarily,

00:58:18.203 --> 00:58:21.372
is really trying to understand
community it's at the very

00:58:21.372 --> 00:58:23.374
micro level of a city.

00:58:23.374 --> 00:58:26.794
And using technology is one
way to be able to do that

00:58:26.794 --> 00:58:29.214
but also being able to embed themselves

00:58:29.214 --> 00:58:32.342
within communities deeply to understand

00:58:32.342 --> 00:58:35.178
the greatest challenges
that people face every scene

00:58:35.178 --> 00:58:36.679
and then being able to use that data

00:58:36.679 --> 00:58:39.015
in a way that can actually affect change.

00:58:39.015 --> 00:58:41.976
- This next generation
cares more about issues

00:58:41.976 --> 00:58:46.523
like the planet, issues
about housing equality

00:58:46.523 --> 00:58:47.857
than previous generations.

00:58:47.857 --> 00:58:50.777
But I think we have to make
government attractive to them.

